#: admin/views/report.php:416
msgid "Severity Levels"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:422
msgid "What do the severity levels mean?"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:428
msgid ""
"Immediate risk of data breach, site takeover, or credential exposure. Act "
"today."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:432
msgid ""
"Significant risk that a motivated attacker could exploit. Address within 24–"
"48 hours."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:436
msgid ""
"Notable weaknesses that increase attack surface. Fix during your next "
"maintenance window."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:440
msgid ""
"Hardening recommendations and best practices. Useful to address but not "
"urgent."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:444
msgid "Status details and observations that require no immediate action."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:452
msgid "Where should I start?"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:456
msgid ""
"Work through findings in severity order — Critical first, then High, then "
"Medium. Each finding has a Recommendation section with clear steps."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:458
msgid "Fix all Critical findings immediately — these are active risks"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:459
msgid "Address High findings within 24–48 hours"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:460
msgid "Schedule Medium fixes in your next maintenance window"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:461
msgid "Work through Low and Info findings as time allows"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:465
msgid ""
"After fixing issues, run a new scan from the Scan page — the report updates "
"only after a fresh scan completes."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:472
msgid "Taking Action"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:478
msgid "A finding mentions a plugin I use — what do I do?"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:482
msgid ""
"Check Dashboard → Updates first — an update may already fix the issue. If an "
"update is available, apply it, then re-run the scan to confirm."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:483
msgid ""
"If no update is available, visit the plugin's page on wordpress.org, open "
"the Support tab, and post a message to the developer asking for a fix "
"timeline. In the meantime, consider deactivating the plugin if the "
"vulnerability is high-severity and you do not rely on it critically."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:490
msgid "How do I fix WordPress core update and integrity issues?"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:494
msgid ""
"For outdated core: go to Dashboard → Updates and click \"Update Now\". This "
"is always safe and takes under 2 minutes."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:495
msgid ""
"For modified or missing core files: go to Dashboard → Updates and click \"Re-"
"install now\" — this re-downloads all core files without touching your "
"content, plugins, or wp-config.php. It is the safest way to restore a clean "
"WordPress installation."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:502
msgid "How do I share this report with my developer?"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:506
msgid ""
"The simplest way is to give your developer temporary Administrator access to "
"this WordPress dashboard so they can view the report directly. If you prefer "
"not to do that:"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:508
msgid ""
"Take a screenshot or use your browser's print function (Ctrl/Cmd+P) to save "
"the report as a PDF"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:509
msgid "Copy the key Critical and High findings and paste them into an email"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:516
msgid "About Results"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:522
msgid "Could any findings be false positives?"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:526
msgid "Yes, occasionally. Some examples:"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:528
msgid ""
"\"No brute-force protection\" may appear even if your host applies firewall-"
"level rate limiting that this plugin cannot detect"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:529
msgid ""
"\"Inactive plugin present\" fires for all inactive plugins, even ones "
"intentionally kept as backups"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:530
msgid ""
"Version disclosure warnings (Low) are informational signals — they are not "
"exploitable on their own"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:532
msgid ""
"Read the description of each finding — it explains why the issue was "
"flagged, giving you the context to judge whether it applies to your setup."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:539
msgid "I fixed an issue — why does it still appear in the report?"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:543
msgid ""
"The report displays results from the last completed scan — it does not "
"update automatically. Go to ShieldScope → Scan, run a new scan, and the "
"updated report will reflect your fixes."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:550
msgid "How do I know when my site is properly secured?"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:554
msgid "A well-secured WordPress site typically shows:"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:556
msgid "Zero Critical or High findings in the report"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:557
msgid "WordPress core, all plugins, and all themes up to date"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:558
msgid "Valid SSL certificate with plenty of days remaining"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:559
msgid "Key security headers (HSTS, CSP, X-Frame-Options) all present"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:561
msgid ""
"Security is an ongoing process, not a one-time task. Regular scans and "
"keeping software updated are the two most effective things you can do."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/report.php:567
msgid ""
"ShieldScope does not change your files, settings, users or content. It "
"stores only its own scan data."
msgstr ""

