#: includes/class-shieldscope-admin.php:94
msgid ""
"Scan results stay in your WordPress database. During a scan ShieldScope "
"sends requests to your own site (to check headers, HTTPS and files that "
"should not be public) and contacts api.wordpress.org (it sends your "
"WordPress version and locale, and the folder names of installed plugins to "
"check whether they are closed or unmaintained) and downloads.wordpress.org "
"(plugin folder names and versions, to compare plugin files with the official "
"release). If you add a WPScan API key it also sends plugin and theme slugs "
"to wpscan.com."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-shieldscope-admin.php:103
msgid ""
"ShieldScope checks WordPress configuration, versions, users, file "
"permissions, server settings and plugin and theme code from inside the site. "
"Findings labelled \"Needs review\" are pattern matches or heuristics that a "
"person must confirm by reading the code; they are not confirmed "
"vulnerabilities. Known-vulnerability results depend on the data source used "
"(a limited built-in list, or WPScan with an API key). It does not test the "
"site from outside like an attacker, does not compare files with a database "
"of known malicious code, and does not monitor the site between scans. A "
"report with no findings does not mean the site is secure."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-shieldscope-admin.php:112
msgid ""
"ShieldScope does not change your files, settings, users or content. It "
"stores only its own data: its settings and scan progress (options), cached "
"lookups (transients), a findings table, and scheduled tasks (one daily clean-"
"up, plus short-lived ones while a scan runs). Deleting the plugin removes "
"all of it."
msgstr ""

